{
  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (RNA-only, singleprofile, PCA-2000) achieves 48.5% acc, while config_010 (image-only, ResNet50) is near-random at 20.8%, indicating RNA is the dominant signal and image contributes little standalone. With only two data points, the most important uncertainty is whether the RNA encoder choice is a meaningful design axis. Question tested: config_004 (RNA-only, singleprofile, scvi_n200) is a controlled comparison to config_001 that changes only the RNA encoder from a linear method (PCA) to a learned nonlinear autoencoder (scVI, 200-dim latent). This isolates whether a structured, biologically-informed representation can outperform variance-maximizing PCA. Why this is the best next experiment: it is the minimal, highest-information change from our current best result. If scVI improves accuracy, it validates learned encoders and justifies further investment in encoder choice (e.g., scGPT) and multimodal fusion with a stronger RNA backbone. If it does not improve, it tells us PCA already captures the relevant variance and the bottleneck lies elsewhere (task difficulty, profile strategy), redirecting budget toward multiprofile aggregation or fusion strategies rather than encoder upgrades."
}

